    "notes": ["Aggregates of the committed preparation reports (reports/qc/<id>/qc_results.json, match_profile.json), STATS across matches. Rates are per player-hour of tracking.", "Optional input for the generator: realistic provider artefacts (jumps, swaps, frozen runs, tracking-area edges, identity inheritance at substitutions) and the event clock offset and dispersion of the annotations (event time minus physical time is about -offset, with the given MAD).", "limit_episodes_per_player_hour: true limit episodes only, player speed above 38 km/h (R-SPEED-LIMIT); ball_limit_episodes: live ball speed above 211 km/h (R-BALL-LIMIT), count per match. Since guidelines v4 neither includes accelerations outside the band.", "Acceleration spikes (R-ACC-BAND, v4): tangential acceleration outside -8.5..+7.5 m/s2 never excludes a sample; a run of at most 0.2 s (5 samples at 25 Hz) is 'noise' (value not used), a longer run is 'approx' (value kept with '≈'). acc_band.per_match and acc_band.pooled give runs, samples, runs per player-hour and mean run length (samples) by class and sign (acceleration > +7.5, deceleration < -8.5); per_match_stats.acc_band_<class>_<sign>_* give their STATS across matches. Pooled rate = total runs / total player-hours. The counts depend on the acceleration filter window (acc_window_samples: 19 samples, chosen by the team after the v4 sensitivity test, R-ACC-WINDOW-TEST). The pipeline derives acceleration from positions, so a generator reproduces these runs as abrupt velocity changes in the positions, never by writing acceleration values. A match without runs of a kind has mean_run_samples null (and is left out of its STATS)."]
